#!/usr/bin/env bash
# worktree-context-hook — Claude Code `SessionStart` hook that DECLARES the Bash
# syntax constraint a `.claude/worktrees` session is about to run into, before
# the first command rather than after it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (#809): a session whose cwd is inside a `.claude/worktrees`
# checkout has its Bash calls screened by the Claude Code binary's worktree
# sandbox. Anything it cannot verify stays inside the worktree comes back
# refused. That rule is stated NOWHERE up front — a scan of every injected
# attachment in the local transcript corpus (hook context, instructions, skill
# listings, system reminders) finds zero statement of it — so it is discoverable
# only by being refused, and it is rediscovered from scratch session after
# session: 252 refusals across 99 transcripts, 54% of every transcript that ever
# runs Bash from a worktree.
#
# This hook does not change the rule and cannot. It only says it out loud.
#
# THE TEXT IS A DECLARATION, NOT THE SANDBOX'S OWN RULES. The rules live in the
# Claude Code binary; `grep -rn 'isolated in the worktree'` over this repo
# returns nothing, so there is no source of truth here to derive them from. The
# list below was written from refusals actually observed, which means it is
# INDICATIVE, NOT EXHAUSTIVE, and it can go stale when the binary changes. The
# text says so itself — a confidently wrong list is worse than a short honest
# one. When a new refusal shape is observed, add it here.
#
# CONTRACT — this must never disrupt a session:
#   * ALWAYS exit 0. For SessionStart, exit 2 BLOCKS the session from starting.
#   * Write to stdout ONLY when the cwd really is a worktree. A SessionStart
#     hook's plain stdout is added to the session context verbatim, so any
#     stray byte here becomes noise in every single session.
#   * Diagnostics, if ever needed, go to stderr.
#
# Not folded into `claude-event-hook`: that shim's contract is the exact
# opposite ("never write to stdout — even plain text is surfaced to the
# session"), and it fires on every session rather than only worktree ones.

# Deliberately NOT `set -e`/`set -o pipefail`: a failure anywhere in here must
# degrade to "no context emitted", never to a nonzero exit that the hooks
# contract would read as a block.
set -u

# Read the SessionStart payload. Claude Code always provides stdin for hooks;
# the timeout guards a misconfigured manual invocation from wedging.
STDIN_JSON=""
if [ ! -t 0 ]; then
    STDIN_JSON="$(timeout 5 cat 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi

# `cwd` is a documented SessionStart field. jq is the only safe way to read it;
# with no jq, fall back to the process cwd, which Claude Code sets to the
# project directory — good enough to answer "am I in a worktree?".
HOOK_CWD=""
if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    HOOK_CWD="$(printf '%s' "$STDIN_JSON" | jq -r '.cwd // empty' 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi
[ -n "$HOOK_CWD" ] || HOOK_CWD="$PWD"

# Only worktree sessions. Every other session gets nothing at all — no stdout,
# no context, no cost.
case "$HOOK_CWD" in
    */.claude/worktrees|*/.claude/worktrees/*) ;;
    *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# Quoted heredoc: nothing in here is expanded by this script. The text is data.
CONTEXT="$(cat <<'CONTEXT_TEXT'
Worktree Bash constraint (declared up front so you do not have to discover it by
being refused).

This session's cwd is inside a .claude/worktrees checkout, so Claude Code
sandboxes Bash: it refuses any command whose effect it cannot verify stays
inside this worktree. Prefer ONE plain command per Bash call, with literal
arguments and absolute paths.

Refusals actually observed from worktree sessions:

  - compound commands - && , || , ; , and pipelines - reported back as "too
    complex to verify"
  - command substitution: $( ... ) and backticks
  - running a string through `source` (or `.`)
  - `git` named in a form too complex to verify, and `git -C <path>` pointing
    outside this worktree
  - `sed` (or any editor) whose script is a runtime-computed value
  - setting GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, refused on a separate "git-config injection"
    ground
  - `gh auth switch`, refused in some sessions as unauthorized persistence but
    not in others
  - a literal `bash` token appearing inside an otherwise plain command

THIS LIST IS INDICATIVE, NOT EXHAUSTIVE. It was written from refusals seen in
practice, not from the sandbox's own rules: those live in the Claude Code binary
and are published nowhere this session can read. Commands it does not mention
are refused too, the exact boundary moves between sessions, and the list can go
stale whenever the binary changes. Treat a refusal as information about the
sandbox, not as a verdict on your intent: decompose into plain single commands
and carry on rather than re-litigating it.
CONTEXT_TEXT
)"

# The documented SessionStart envelope. jq builds it so the text is escaped
# correctly whatever it contains. Without jq, plain stdout from a SessionStart
# hook is added to the context anyway, so the fallback still lands.
if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    jq -n --arg ctx "$CONTEXT" \
        '{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"SessionStart","additionalContext":$ctx}}' \
        2>/dev/null || printf '%s\n' "$CONTEXT"
else
    printf '%s\n' "$CONTEXT"
fi

exit 0
